# SECTION 3: COMPLEX SYNTAX AND GRAMMATICAL STRUCTURE NOTES
# ======================================================================================

# Subtopic A: Inversion Structure (Causative & Emphatic)
# - Definition: Placing an auxiliary verb before the subject for emphasis or poetic effect.
# - Trigger Words: Seldom, rarely, hardly, scarcely, never, only under, little.
# - Standard Form: "I have rarely seen such dedication."
# - Inverted Form: "Rarely have I seen such dedication."
# - Advanced Form: "Not only did she complete the assignment ahead of schedule, but 
#   she also incorporated supplementary empirical data."

# Subtopic B: Subjunctive Mood
# - Definition: A grammatical mood used to express wishes, hypothetical situations, 
#   demands, or suggestions.
# - Rule: Always use the base form of the verb after requirement/demand verbs (demand, 
#   insist, suggest, recommend, require) regardless of the subject pronoun.
# - Example: "The board of directors insisted that the CEO resign immediately." 
#   (Notice: 'resign' instead of 'resigns' or 'resigned').

# Subtopic C: Mixed Conditionals
# - Definition: Combining a past counterfactual condition with a present result, or 
#   vice versa.
# - Formula: If + Past Perfect (Type 3 condition), would + Present Simple/Continuous (Type 2 result).
# - Example: "If she had accepted the fellowship in London last year (past action), she 
#   would be living abroad right now (present state)."


