# Note 1: Word Stress Shifts (Noun vs. Verb pairs)
# - Many English two-syllable words change stress based on their grammatical category:
#   * RE-cord (Noun: a permanent account) vs. re-CORD (Verb: to register or write down)
#   * PRE-sent (Noun/Adjective: a gift or current) vs. pre-SENT (Verb: to introduce/show)
#   * OB-ject (Noun: a tangible thing) vs. ob-JECT (Verb: to express opposition)

# Note 2: Silent Letters and Historical Etymologies
# - 'Knight': The 'k' and 'gh' were originally fully pronounced in Old English/Germanic 
#   roots ('cniht'), but shifted during the Great Vowel Shift and standardization phases.
# - 'Subtle': The 'b' is a Latinate insertion from 'subtilis', retained in spelling 
#   despite dropping out of standard pronunciation over centuries.

